  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n* Strong, sudden northeast wind shift this evening across\n  northwest Montana, including Flathead Lake. \n\n* Thunderstorms capable of 50+ mph wind gusts across southwest\n  Montana and extreme northwest Montana, late-afternoon and\n  evening.\n\n\nFire Weather, Air Quality, and Northeast Wind Today/Tonight:\n\nSmoke has once again pooled into valley areas this morning as\nsharp inversions have developed overnight under clear skies. GOES\nSatellite imagery shows monsoon moisture beginning to stream into\nmid-upper levels of the atmosphere as south-southwest flow has \ndeveloped aloft. Warm and dry conditions are expected in lower \nportions of the atmosphere through this afternoon, with late \nafternoon gusty winds leading to elevated fire weather concerns, \nespecially across southwest Montana. The afternoon winds may \nprovide localized temporary relief from highly concentrated \nwildfire smoke. Confidence has increased for a strong and sudden \nnortheast wind shift this evening across northwest Montana, with \ngusts of 30-40 mph across the Flathead Valley, Flathead Lake, and \nwestern foothills of the Mission Mountains. A lake wind advisory \nhas been issued as this wind shift will bring hazardous \nconditions for those with plans on outdoor lakes this evening. \n\nThunderstorms Return:\n\nMonsoon moisture will continue to stream into the region, opening\nthe door for high based showers and thunderstorms by mid-afternoon\nthrough the evening. Warm and dry sub-cloud layers will promote mainly\ndry thunderstorms capable of 40+ mph wind gusts over southwest \nMontana and across extreme northwest Montana. Little appreciable \nrainfall is expected with the Wednesday storms, however moisture \nwill become much more available for subsequent days through the \nweekend. In fact, a marginal risk of excessive rainfall arrives as\nsoon as Thursday, with recent and ongoing wildfires having the \nhighest risk of runoff and flood impacts. The presence of wildfire\nsmoke will be a bit of wild card in the efficiency of \nthunderstorm development.\n\nBigger pattern change to come?\n\nLong range model ensembles suggest that a low pressure trough will \npersist off coastal western US next week. Most pattern solutions \nfeature either southwest flow aloft or a ridge of high pressure over \nthe Northern Rockies. Either way, the moisture from tropical storms \nin the Pacific Ocean will play a factor in whether temperatures \nreturn to summertime warmth or not. Wildfire smoke is still a big \nquestion mark next week. Overall, the pattern will be moving away\nfrom elevated to critical fire weather concerns (hot, dry, windy\npattern) that we've had consistently for many days.",
